#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Decide whether the standing skill-registry PR may be MERGED (.github#642, epic #266).

`skill-registry-autofix.yml` reconciles the mechanical drift and opens a standing PR. #640 fixed the
reason that PR could never go GREEN (its own coherence check was failed by the judgement findings the
bot correctly refused to touch). But a green PR that nobody merges lands nothing: #595 went green and
then SAT there, while `main` stayed red on the eight stale digests it had already fixed. The bot was
an ornament; #640 only made it a green one.

WHY THIS IS NOT `gh pr merge --auto` (the org's other two propagation arms), MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED

`coordination-propagate.yml` and `build-config-propagate.yml` both arm GitHub's NATIVE auto-merge and
say why: "push + a red pull-gate is not enforcement". #642 proposed copying that arm a third time. It
cannot be copied, for two reasons — and the second is the one that matters.

  1. `.github` has `allow_auto_merge: false`. `gh pr merge --auto` fails outright here, so a verbatim
     copy of the propagate arm would take its `autoMergeAllowed != true` branch and `exit 1` EVERY
     DAY, telling a human to go flip a repo setting. That is not enforcement either — it is a new
     permanently-red check, which is precisely what the comment it was copied from says trains
     everyone to ignore checks. This one is merely a nuisance; a setting could be flipped.

  2. THE DECISIVE ONE. `registry-coherence` — the check that decides whether this PR is CORRECT — is
     NOT a required check on `main`. The required set is `contract-coherence / coherence`,
     `projection`, `roster-closure`, `drift`, `reconcile`. GitHub's native auto-merge waits for
     REQUIRED checks and merges straight past a red NON-required one.

     So native auto-merge would ignore the single check whose redness means "this snapshot is
     OBSOLETE — merging it writes superseded digests over the registry and REDs the very gate it was
     opened to green". That is #425's inversion, and arming `--auto` would not fix the bot; it would
     AUTOMATE the inversion. The one signal that separates a good reconcile from a poisonous one is
     the one signal `--auto` cannot see.

AND `registry-coherence` MUST NOT SIMPLY BE MADE REQUIRED. Its verdict is a function of OTHER REPOS'
`main`s (it re-digests producer bodies). Requiring it would mean a producer merging a skill-body edit
flips it red on every open `.github` PR and BLOCKS them all until the next 06:42 autofix run lands —
an external repo's merge deadlocking this repo's merges, which is exactly the hazard
`check-required-contexts.py` (#549) exists to document. It has to stay non-required. Therefore native
auto-merge can never be safe on this PR, and the mechanism has to be a STRONGER one, not a copied one.

WHAT THIS GATE DOES INSTEAD — a merge that respects EVERY check, not merely the required ones

This is strictly stronger than `--auto`: it needs no repo setting, no branch-protection change, and it
refuses on a red NON-required check, which is the case `--auto` structurally cannot refuse. Branch
protection still applies underneath — a direct merge is refused by GitHub exactly as `--auto` would
be — so this ADDS a gate rather than routing around one.

REFUSE UNLESS ALL FIVE HOLD

  1. THE CHECKS WERE READ AT ALL. No parseable payload => the API read failed => refuse. Same shape as
     the retire gate: the evidence is the payload, never an exit code.

  2. THE SUBJECT EXISTS. ZERO check runs is a FINDING, not a pass (#606). "Every check passed" and "CI
     never started" are the same empty set, and only one of them is safe. A conflicted PR gets no
     `refs/pull/N/merge`, so no workflow ever starts and the head SHA has zero check runs FOREVER —
     merging on that emptiness is the epic-#266 fail-open in its purest form.

  3. `registry-coherence` IS PRESENT, BY NAME. #606 applied to the one check that actually decides
     this PR. If the coherence gate did not report — a renamed job, a narrowed `paths:` filter, a
     workflow that failed to schedule — then the reconcile was NEVER VERIFIED, and the other checks
     going green says nothing about the digests in the diff. An absent subject reads identically to a
     passing one in any "are all checks green?" test, which is why it is asserted by NAME and not left
     to the aggregate. This is the assertion `--auto` cannot make, and the reason this script exists.

  4. NOTHING IS STILL RUNNING. A pending check has not passed. Refuse; the next run re-pushes and
     re-gates. Latency is not a failure mode — merging early is.

  5. NOTHING IS RED. `success` / `skipped` / `neutral` are green; everything else (`failure`,
     `cancelled`, `timed_out`, `action_required`, `stale`, a null conclusion on a completed run) is
     not. A red check is a finding, not an obstacle to route around — INCLUDING a non-required one.

     ...EXCEPT A SUPERSEDED ONE, AND ONLY A SUPERSEDED ONE (#700). A `cancelled` run that a LATER run
     of ITS OWN CONCURRENCY GROUP replaced is not a verdict about this PR — it is a run that was
     stopped so a fresher one could answer. Counting it red refuses the merge on evidence that has
     already been superseded by evidence we ARE counting.

     THIS BOT MANUFACTURES THAT STATE ON EVERY RECONCILE, so the refusal was not a rare interleaving
     — it was the standing-PR path. `skill-registry-autofix.yml` force-pushes the branch (a
     `synchronize` event) and then edits the PR body (an `edited` event). Both are `pull_request`
     events, so for both `github.ref` is `refs/pull/N/merge` — ONE `cancel-in-progress` concurrency
     group — and the `edited` run cancels the still-running `synchronize` run at the SAME head SHA.
     The head SHA is left carrying `cancelled` check runs that a later, successful run of the same
     workflow already replaced. This gate called them red, so the bot refused to merge the PR it had
     just pushed, and `main` stayed stale until a human merged by hand — the exact "pushed, then SAT
     there" failure the header above says this workflow was built to end.

WHAT SUPERSESSION IS KEYED ON, AND WHY NOT THE OBVIOUS THING (#698, measured — do not "simplify" this)

  NOT the check-run NAME. "Latest check run per name" is what branch protection does, and here it
  FAILS OPEN: check-run `.name` is the JOB name, and job names COLLIDE ACROSS WORKFLOWS. Measured on
  this repo: SEVEN check runs named `fixture`, from SIX different workflows (`pin-coherence`,
  `projection-selftest`, `permission-coherence`, `timeout-coherence`, …). Collapse those by name and
  a genuinely FAILING `fixture`, followed by another workflow's successful `fixture`, reports "all
  green" and merges. That hides a RED check — #606's signature, in the direction that lands a bad
  commit.

  NOT the workflow `.path` alone either. `cancel-in-progress` only cancels within
  `group: <workflow>-${{ github.ref }}` — same workflow AND SAME REF. A `workflow_dispatch` run on
  the same branch shares the SHA and the path and carries a HIGHER `run_number` (the counter is
  per-workflow, across every event), but a DIFFERENT `github.ref` — so it supersedes nothing. Key on
  `.path` alone and it licenses the drop anyway: `closing-keywords.yml` gates its real job on
  `if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'`, so its dispatch run SKIPS that job and still concludes
  `success` — dropping a cancelled PR run in its favour would count a vacuous green.

  SO: the CONCURRENCY GROUP — (workflow path, event, head branch, the PR set) — which is the thing
  `cancel-in-progress` actually keys on. A cancelled run is dropped ONLY when a run with the SAME
  group and a HIGHER `run_number` exists.

  AND THE DROP RULE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW, so that every way it can be wrong is a REFUSAL:
    * only `cancelled` is ever dropped. A `failure` is NEVER dropped, whatever it is named and
      whatever replaced it. A cancelled run that NOBODY re-ran is still a finding.
    * no workflow-runs payload (absent, unreadable, a failed API read) => NOTHING is superseded =>
      every cancelled run stays RED. Being unable to read the runs cannot license a merge; it can
      only refuse one.
    * a check run that joins to no workflow run keeps its verdict. That is what makes a NON-ACTIONS
      check (a third-party app) safe here: it has no workflow run, so it can never be dropped. This
      gate reads CHECK RUNS as its subject and uses workflow runs ONLY as evidence of supersession,
      which is why it needs no "Actions-only" assertion of the kind the /pnext-item recipe carries.

  ...and the PR's own merge state must be CLEAN or HAS_HOOKS. BLOCKED (a required check or review is
  missing), UNSTABLE (mergeable, but something is red), DIRTY/BEHIND (needs the rebase the next run's
  force-push provides), and UNKNOWN (GitHub has not computed it) all refuse. Read from the PR rather
  than from branch protection, for the reason #463 established the hard way: the protection endpoint
  needs `administration: read`, and a 403 there is neither a verdict nor an absence of one.

SEQUENCING AGAINST THE RETIRE GATE — the part #642 flags as more than copy-paste

They cannot race, and not by luck: MERGE hangs off `steps.diff.outputs.changed == 'true'` and RETIRE
hangs off `steps.diff.outputs.changed == 'false'`. They are mutually exclusive branches of one
boolean, so no run can take both, and the `concurrency` group serialises runs so no two runs can
interleave them. Across runs the two remain disjoint by construction: a PR this gate MERGED is closed,
so no later run can retire it; a PR a later run RETIRES is one whose diff is empty, which is precisely
the state in which this gate is never consulted. `tests/skill-registry` case 31 pins the mutual
exclusion structurally, so a future edit cannot quietly move them onto the same branch.

Exit 0 = merge allowed. Non-zero = do not merge, and the reason is on stderr.
Emits `merge=` / `checks=` on stdout for $GITHUB_OUTPUT.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import pathlib
import sys

# A completed check run that is not one of these has not passed. `neutral` and `skipped` are green by
# GitHub's own reckoning (a path-filtered job reports `skipped`), and `coordination-propagate.yml`'s
# classifier draws the line in exactly the same place — deliberately, so the org has ONE answer to
# "what counts as green?" rather than two that disagree at the edges (#485).
GREEN = {"success", "skipped", "neutral"}

# The PR is mergeable and every gate GitHub itself enforces is satisfied. Everything else refuses; see
# the docstring for why each of the others is a refusal and not a retry.
MERGEABLE = {"CLEAN", "HAS_HOOKS"}

# The check whose verdict IS the correctness of this PR. Not required on `main` (and must not be — see
# the docstring), so nothing but this gate will ever look at it.
SUBJECT = "registry-coherence"


def _flatten(payload: object) -> list[dict]:
    """Accept `gh api .../check-runs --paginate --slurp` (a list of PAGES) or a flat list of runs.

    The slurped shape is `[{"check_runs": [...]}, ...]` — one object per page. Flattening it HERE
    rather than in a jq expression in the YAML is deliberate: `--paginate` is the difference between
    reading every check and reading the first 30 of them, and a truncated read does not look truncated
    — it looks like an answer (#547). The parsing that depends on it belongs somewhere a test can drive
    it.
    """
    if isinstance(payload, dict):  # a single un-slurped page
        payload = [payload]
    if not isinstance(payload, list):
        raise ValueError(f"expected a list of check-run pages, got {type(payload).__name__}")
    runs: list[dict] = []
    for page in payload:
        if isinstance(page, dict) and "check_runs" in page:
            runs.extend(page["check_runs"])
        elif isinstance(page, dict):  # already a flat list of check runs
            runs.append(page)
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"not a check run or a page of them: {type(page).__name__}")
    return runs


def _flatten_runs(payload: object) -> list[dict]:
    """Accept `gh api .../actions/runs?head_sha=… --paginate --slurp` (a list of PAGES) or a flat list.

    Same shape and the same reason as `_flatten`: `--paginate` is the difference between reading every
    workflow run and reading the first 30, and a truncated read does not look truncated (#547). Here a
    truncated read is merely conservative — a run we never saw cannot supersede anything, so the worst
    it does is leave a cancelled check RED — but "conservative by accident" is not a property to rely
    on, so it pages, and this parses what paging produces.
    """
    if isinstance(payload, dict):
        payload = [payload]
    if not isinstance(payload, list):
        raise ValueError(f"expected a list of workflow-run pages, got {type(payload).__name__}")
    runs: list[dict] = []
    for page in payload:
        if isinstance(page, dict) and "workflow_runs" in page:
            runs.extend(page["workflow_runs"])
        elif isinstance(page, dict):
            runs.append(page)
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"not a workflow run or a page of them: {type(page).__name__}")
    return runs


def _concurrency_group(run: dict) -> tuple:
    """The group `cancel-in-progress` actually keys on: `<workflow>-${{ github.ref }}`.

    Matching it EXACTLY is what stops the drop rule being a hole — see the docstring. `.path` is the
    workflow (`.name` is the JOB name and collides across workflows); the event + head branch + PR set
    stand in for `github.ref`, which the runs API does not hand back directly. A `workflow_dispatch`
    run on the same branch therefore lands in a DIFFERENT group from the `pull_request` run it would
    otherwise have been allowed to supersede.
    """
    prs = sorted(pr.get("number") for pr in (run.get("pull_requests") or []) if isinstance(pr, dict))
    return (run.get("path"), run.get("event"), run.get("head_branch"), tuple(prs))


def superseded_suites(runs: list[dict]) -> set:
    """-> the `check_suite_id`s of CANCELLED workflow runs that a later run of their OWN group replaced.

    Narrow on purpose. A run is superseded only if it was `cancelled` AND another run shares its
    concurrency group AND carries a higher `run_number`. Everything else — a failure, a cancelled run
    nobody re-ran, a run whose group nothing else touched — is not superseded and keeps its verdict.
    """
    highest: dict[tuple, int] = {}
    for run in runs:
        number = run.get("run_number")
        if not isinstance(number, int):
            continue
        group = _concurrency_group(run)
        if number > highest.get(group, -1):
            highest[group] = number

    suites = set()
    for run in runs:
        if (run.get("conclusion") or "") != "cancelled":
            continue  # a FAILURE is never dropped, whatever replaced it
        number = run.get("run_number")
        suite = run.get("check_suite_id")
        if not isinstance(number, int) or suite is None:
            continue  # cannot prove it was replaced => it stays red
        if highest.get(_concurrency_group(run), -1) > number:
            suites.add(suite)
    return suites


def _suite_of(run: dict):
    """The check suite a CHECK RUN belongs to — `.check_suite.id`, the join key to a workflow run."""
    suite = run.get("check_suite")
    if isinstance(suite, dict) and suite.get("id") is not None:
        return suite["id"]
    return run.get("check_suite_id")


def decide(raw: str, merge_state: str, runs_raw: str = "") -> tuple[bool, str, int]:
    """-> (merge, reason, n_checks). `merge` is "this PR is safe to land, on every check we can see"."""
    try:
        runs = _flatten(json.loads(raw))
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — any failure to parse means we did not read the checks
        return False, (
            f"the check-run payload did not parse ({exc.__class__.__name__}) — so the checks were "
            "NOT read. A failed API read is not an absence of red checks; it is an absence of "
            "evidence, and merging on it would be merging blind."
        ), 0

    # SUPERSESSION (#700). Resolved from the WORKFLOW-RUNS payload, because supersession is a fact
    # about a workflow run and check runs cannot express it. Unreadable or absent => `suites` is empty
    # => nothing is dropped => every cancelled check stays RED. This read can only ever REFUSE a merge
    # that would otherwise have happened; it can never license one, which is why its failure is silent
    # rather than fatal (a gate that goes permanently red is a gate everyone learns to ignore).
    suites: set = set()
    runs_read = False
    if runs_raw.strip():
        try:
            suites = superseded_suites(_flatten_runs(json.loads(runs_raw)))
            runs_read = True
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — no supersession evidence; every cancelled run stays red
            suites = set()

    def is_superseded(r: dict) -> bool:
        # ONLY `cancelled`, and ONLY into a suite a later run of its own group replaced. Anything else
        # — a failure, a pending run, a check run that joins to no workflow run (a non-Actions app) —
        # keeps its verdict and is judged below.
        return (r.get("conclusion") or "") == "cancelled" and _suite_of(r) in suites

    dropped = sorted(r.get("name") or "?" for r in runs if is_superseded(r))
    runs = [r for r in runs if not is_superseded(r)]
    superseded_note = (
        f" ({len(dropped)} superseded run(s) dropped: {', '.join(dropped)} — cancelled, and re-run by "
        "a later run of their own concurrency group)" if dropped else ""
    )

    # (2) ZERO CHECKS IS NOT A PASS (#606). This is the same empty set as "everything passed", and the
    # commonest cause is a CONFLICTED PR: GitHub cannot build `refs/pull/N/merge`, so no workflow ever
    # starts and this stays empty forever. No amount of waiting fixes it, and merging is catastrophic.
    if not runs:
        if dropped:
            # Every check we had was a superseded one, so the REPLACEMENT runs have not registered
            # their check runs yet. Refusing is still right — we have nothing green to merge on — but
            # this is a "come back in 20s", not the conflicted-PR diagnosis below, and saying the wrong
            # one sends the next reader off rebasing a PR that was never conflicted.
            return False, (
                f"every check run on this PR was SUPERSEDED{superseded_note}, and the runs that "
                "replaced them have not reported yet. Nothing is left to judge, so there is nothing "
                "green to merge on. Not a conflict — the next poll sees the replacement runs."
            ), 0
        return False, (
            "the PR has ZERO check runs — CI never started, so nothing has passed. That is a FINDING, "
            "not a pass: an empty check list is indistinguishable from a green one to any naive "
            "aggregate (#606). The usual cause is a conflicted PR (no `refs/pull/N/merge`, so no "
            "workflow is ever scheduled); the next run force-pushes a rebased branch."
        ), 0

    names = {r.get("name") for r in runs}

    # (3) THE SUBJECT MUST EXIST, BY NAME. The reason this whole script exists rather than a `--auto`.
    if SUBJECT not in names:
        return False, (
            f"`{SUBJECT}` did not report on this PR — the check that decides whether the reconcile is "
            f"CORRECT is absent, so the reconcile was never verified. The other {len(runs)} check(s) "
            "going green says nothing about the digests in this diff. An absent subject reads exactly "
            "like a passing one in any 'are all checks green?' test (#606), so it is asserted by name. "
            f"Has the job been renamed, or the workflow's `paths:` filter narrowed?{superseded_note}"
        ), len(runs)

    # (4) NOTHING STILL RUNNING. A pending check has not passed.
    pending = sorted(r.get("name") or "?" for r in runs if r.get("status") != "completed")
    if pending:
        return False, (
            f"{len(pending)} check(s) have not completed ({', '.join(pending)}). A pending check has "
            f"not passed. Refusing to merge; the next run re-pushes and re-gates.{superseded_note}"
        ), len(runs)

    # (5) NOTHING RED — INCLUDING THE NON-REQUIRED ONES. This is the case `--auto` cannot refuse, and
    # `registry-coherence` is the non-required check it would have merged straight past.
    red = sorted(
        f"{r.get('name')}={r.get('conclusion') or 'null'}"
        for r in runs
        if (r.get("conclusion") or "") not in GREEN
    )
    if red:
        # A `cancelled` still in this list is one we could NOT prove superseded. Say which — "no later
        # run of its group re-ran it" and "we never read the workflow runs" are different facts, and a
        # gate that names the wrong one sends the reader hunting a failure that does not exist.
        blind = (
            " NOTE: no workflow-runs payload was read, so NO run could be proven superseded — a "
            "`cancelled` above may be a run this bot's own `edited` event replaced (#700). That read "
            "is evidence for DROPPING a check, never for merging one, so its absence refuses rather "
            "than merges; but pass `--runs` and the refusal may resolve on its own."
            if not runs_read and any(r.endswith("=cancelled") for r in red) else ""
        )
        return False, (
            f"{len(red)} check(s) are not green ({', '.join(red)}). A red check is a finding, not an "
            f"obstacle to route around — and that holds for the NON-required ones too. If `{SUBJECT}` "
            "is among them, this snapshot is OBSOLETE: merging it would write superseded digests over "
            "the registry and RED the gate it was opened to green (#425). GitHub's native auto-merge "
            f"would have landed it anyway, which is why this gate exists.{superseded_note}{blind}"
        ), len(runs)

    # ...and GitHub's own view of the PR must agree. Asked of the PR, never of branch protection (#463).
    if merge_state not in MERGEABLE:
        return False, (
            f"every check is green, but the PR's mergeStateStatus is '{merge_state}', not CLEAN. "
            "BLOCKED = a required check or review is missing; UNSTABLE = something is red that the "
            "rollup above did not show; BEHIND/DIRTY = the branch needs the rebase the next run's "
            "force-push provides; UNKNOWN = GitHub has not computed mergeability yet. None of these "
            "is a state to merge in."
        ), len(runs)

    return True, (
        f"all {len(runs)} check(s) are green — including `{SUBJECT}`, which is NOT a required check and "
        f"which GitHub's native auto-merge would therefore have ignored — and the PR is {merge_state}. "
        "The reconcile is verified against the producers it was computed from. Safe to land."
        f"{superseded_note}"
    ), len(runs)


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--checks", required=True,
                    help="JSON from `gh api .../check-runs --paginate --slurp`")
    ap.add_argument("--merge-state", default="",
                    help="mergeStateStatus from `gh pr view --json mergeStateStatus`")
    ap.add_argument("--runs", default="",
                    help="JSON from `gh api .../actions/runs?head_sha=SHA --paginate --slurp`. Evidence "
                         "of SUPERSESSION, and nothing else: it can only ever DROP a cancelled check "
                         "run that a later run of its own concurrency group replaced (#700). Omit it "
                         "and every cancelled run stays RED — never the other way round.")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    path = pathlib.Path(args.checks)
    raw = path.read_text() if path.exists() else ""
    # An absent `--runs` file reads as "" — no supersession evidence — which is the SAME conservative
    # state as omitting the flag: nothing is dropped, every cancelled run stays red. It is deliberately
    # not an error, because a failed `gh api` leaves exactly this behind and the safe response to it is
    # a refusal we can explain, not a crash the workflow reports as an infrastructure failure.
    runs_path = pathlib.Path(args.runs) if args.runs else None
    runs_raw = runs_path.read_text() if runs_path and runs_path.exists() else ""
    merge, reason, n = decide(raw, args.merge_state.strip().upper(), runs_raw)

    print(f"merge={'true' if merge else 'false'}")
    print(f"checks={n}")
    print(("MERGE-OK: " if merge else "NOT MERGING: ") + reason, file=sys.stderr)

    return 0 if merge else 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
